AD  - Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical and Photonics Engineering, Kgs. Lyngby, Denmark
AB  - Low-power wide-area networks (LPWANs) enable energy-efficient long-range communication, but their limited throughput makes transmission of high-entropy data such as voice challenging. We present a complete system for asynchronous, bidirectional voice messaging over LoRaWAN, using Codec2 speech compression and packet-level fragmentation to send multi-second audio. A hardware prototype based on an ESP32 + SX1276 performs on-device sampling, encoding, and playback, while a backend running on Helium's LoRaWAN infrastructure supports message routing and asynchronous downlink retrieval via periodic ping frames. We evaluate transmission time under duty-cycle constraints and analyze realworld operating cost, showing that low-bitrate audio messages can be delivered within minutes at low recurring cost. The design illustrates the feasibility of LoRaWAN for communication modalities beyond scalar telemetry. This addresses the growing need for redundant, off-grid communication layers, with potential applications for users with limited technological proficiency, or in remote and disaster-affected regions where cellular infrastructure is compromised. However, message delivery latency remains on the order of minutes and audio quality is limited by the available bitrate, making the system suitable for specialized use cases rather than conventional voice communication. © 2026 IEEE.
