AD  - New Prince Shri Bhavani College of Engineering and Technology, Department of Electronics and Communication Engineering, India
AD  - New Prince Shri Bhavani College of Engineering and Technology, Department Electronics and Communication Engineering, India
AB  - Maritime Okay, so even with all these surveillance systems along the coast, safety and navigation are still big concerns. Especially in those remote coastal and offshore areas where one cannot keep an eye on everything all the time. This article highlights a smart buoy system that uses AI and IoT technology to help with navigation and warn people of any dangers in real-time. The system uses a Raspberry Pi with a 5-inch touch screen to facilitate user interaction. It also uses an Arduino Nano-based controller to effectively control hardware components. The GPS module is the most critical component in identifying the exact location. LoRa is used for communication, which is very effective over long distances and is also very reliable. It is solar-powered, meaning it can run 24/7 without having to change the battery. The buoy also has danger alerting systems such as an LCD display, buzzer, and SMS notification to warn people of any dangers. It is intelligent enough to locate itself and its surroundings. It can detect events like unusual movement, collisions, or other hazards and send real-time notifications to remote control centers. The system has been tested and proved to be able to track GPS positions correctly, communicate effectively, and send hazard notifications on time. This means that it really makes a difference in maritime safety. The device is small, with low power consumption, and is priced low enough to be reused for other purposes like ship routing, accident prevention, coastal monitoring, and border protection. The smart buoy system is an example of how AI and IoT can revolutionize maritime safety. It operates continuously through solar power, has LoRa for long-range communication, and provides real-time notifications to ensure continuous monitoring of the waters, even in remote locations. It can also be expanded and repurposed for other uses such as coastal monitoring, border protection, and navigation assistance. Makes as wonder how many accidents could be avoided if more places had something like this. The user interface based on Raspberry Pi and the control system based on Arduino Nano guarantee that the system is computationally efficient and dependable. © 2026 IEEE.
