AD  - Software Engineering Institute, East China Normal University, Shanghai, 200062, China
AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable generality and creativity across various domains but also expose significant security risks. Existing defense strategies often rely on strengthened rejection mechanisms to reduce the attack success rate (ASR), which, however, typically comes at the cost of model availability. This paper proposes a method called Dual-Responder and Dynamic Dual-Routing (DR2), which trains an availability responder and a safety responder in parallel on the same base model. Lightweight routing modules are attached to the middle and final hidden layers of the model to enable adaptive security perception and generation tendency control for different input instructions. Experiments on GPT-2, LLaMA, and DeepSeek validate the effectiveness of the proposed method, showing that DR2 achieves a substantial reduction in ASR while maintaining high availability. With minimal structural overhead, the DR2 framework achieves a dynamic balance between safety and availability, demonstrating its universality and efficiency across multiple models and attack scenarios. This work provides a transferable and interpretable solution for security control in trustworthy large language models. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
