AB  - Mining is one of the most unsafe trades because it is not visible, gases are poisonous, structural breakdowns, and slow response to emergencies in the underground conditions. The safety of the workers will be ensured by the real-time monitoring and real-time communication systems. A Smart Helmet is discussed in this paper, which aims at improving the safety of underground miners by constantly tracking the environmental factors and the condition of the workers. The system combines MQ-series gas sensors used to sense such toxic gases as methane and carbon monoxide, a BMP280 pressure sensor used to determine the underground depth, and a GPS module used to track the real-time positioning. All sensor data is processed by an ESP32 microcontroller, and the sensor transmits all sensor data via LoRa-based mesh network to a central control station, which can receive the data even in strongly poor-connection tunnels. When the helmet identifies any dangerous situation or irregular depth, the wearer can be alerted immediately by buzzers and lights, as well as emergency information will be transmitted to the control room, and timely rescue operations can be performed. The system is lean and its energy consuming, economical, and can run on its own. This Smart Helmet can greatly increase safety of occupations, prevent the risk of any accidents and increase the efficiency of working conditions during underground mining operations by offering real-time monitoring, instant alerts and long-range communication. © 2026 IEEE.
