UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033704417?origin=resultslist
AD  - Pondicherry University, School of Engineering and Technology, Department of Computer Science, Pondicherry, India
AD  - Pondicherry University Community College, Department of Computer Science, Lawspet, Pondicherry, India
AB  - In recent years, trust-based clustering and routing has emerged as an effective approach for enhancing security and energy efficiency in the context of Wireless Sensor Networks (WSNs), Mobile Ad Hoc Networks (MANETs) and Internet of Things (IoT). By combining trust evaluation with clustering and routing mechanisms, these methods ensure secure data delivery by isolating malicious nodes. The optimization algorithms such as Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO), Genetic Algorithm (GA), Bacteria Foraging Optimization (BFO) and Crow Search (CS) effectively enabled intelligent cluster head selection and energy-aware routing, whereas Machine Learning (ML) and Deep Learning (DL) techniques introduced predictive trust modelling and adaptive decision making. With rising adoption of Low Power Wide Area Network (LPWAN) technologies like LoRaWAN, combination of trust-based optimization becomes required for ensuring secure, long-range and energy-constrained communications in IoT deployments. Despite these advancements, existing models face challenges like high computational overhead, difficulty in handling dynamic topologies, limited scalability and vulnerability to sophisticated attacks. This survey presents survey of recent optimization-driven and learning-assisted trust-based clustering and routing approaches by highlighting their methodologies, performance benefits and limitations for large-scale real-time deployments.  © 2025 IEEE.
