AB  - Lightweight Internet of Things (IoT) devices - microcontroller-class nodes with less than 512-kB RAM, sub-100-MHz clocks, and low-power radios [Bluetooth low energy (BLE), Zigbee, LoRa, and narrowband IoT (NB-IoT)] - are now widely deployed in settings, where traditional security stacks are infeasible. This survey examines how artificial intelligence (AI) can harden such constrained platforms against device-, network-, and application-layer threats, including spoofing, routing manipulation, DDoS, malware, and advanced persistent threats (APTs). We: 1) formalize a lightweight envelope that bounds feasible defenses in terms of RAM, CPU, bandwidth, and energy; 2) consolidate protocol-side risks across BLE, Zigbee, and LoRaWAN; and 3) review deployable AI techniques through a deployment-first lens that separates training [edge, cloud, and federated learning (FL)] from on-device inference. Distinct from prior surveys, we provide resource-annotated comparisons that report accuracy alongside model size, peak RAM, latency, and estimated energy per inference, showing how pruning, posttraining quantization, distillation, and feature narrowing shift feasibility on microcontroller unit (MCU) targets. Covered methods include compact classifiers [linear models, trees, and support vector machine (SVM)], quantized TinyCNN/TinyRNN, and graph-based intrusion detection (IDS), reinforcement learning (RL) for adaptive rate limiting and channel selection, and privacy-preserving FL with update compression. We conclude with a pragmatic agenda - energy-adaptive inference, low-power wide-area network (LPWAN)-aware scheduling and FL, robustness to poisoning and evasion, and reproducible benchmarks that couple accuracy with size/latency/energy on real hardware - aimed at making AI-based security practical at scale for lightweight IoT deployments.  © 2014 IEEE.
