AB  - Modern Controller Area Network (CAN) buses lack native security, leaving vehicles exposed to spoofing, replay, and injection attacks, especially zero-day or unseen variants that evade traditional IDSs. We present CANalyze-AI, an edge-optimized hybrid IDS combining Random Forest and XGBoost with a 4-bit, LoRA-adapted GPT-2 to add semantic reasoning under strict resource budgets. Upon flagging anomalous 50-frame windows, the LLM produces concise, human-readable rationales and drafts Sigma rules that pass schema checks before use. On a composite CAN dataset, CANalyze-AI completes detection-plus-explanation in under 100 ms per window, fits within a ≤4 GB RAM envelope, and improves F1 by +0.9% over XGBoost and +2.2% over Random Forest. Under evasion, true-positive rate degrades by 7.1%, as compared to ≥12% for the baselines. Ablations show adaptive routing and LoRA adapters are key to performance and interpretability. We discuss practical guardrails against prompt-level attacks and limits arising from synthetic “zero-day” generation, and outline paths to real-fleet validation. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2026.
