AB  - The successful deployment of Advanced Metering Infrastructure (AMI) is highly dependent on the selection of the right communication technology, which should be tailored to the geographical and operational characteristics of the target area. This study presents a structured decision-making framework to optimize communication technology selection for AMI by applying the Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP). The analysis incorporates both technical and non-technical evaluation criteria, including communication performance metrics (e.g., bandwidth, data rate, latency, coverage, and reliability) and cost-related aspects (e.g., installation, operational, and maintenance costs), in addition to data security considerations. The assessment focused on three different regional classifications - urban-commercial, rural, and industrial - each with unique infrastructure readiness and AMI functionality requirements. Expert judgment was used to assign relative weights to each criterion through pairwise comparisons using Expert Choice software. The resulting priority weights were applied to evaluate various communication technology alternatives, such as Wi-Fi, NB-IoT, LoRa, RF Mesh, PLC, and cellular technologies. The findings revealed that Wi-Fi is best suited for urban commercial areas, NB-IoT for rural areas, and cellular networks for industrial zones. These results provide strategic insights for service providers and policy makers to ensure efficient, reliable, and context-appropriate deployment of AMI communications across different geographical locations.  © 2025 IEEE.
