TI  - Multimodal Framework for Detecting Malicious QR Codes in Social Engineering Attacks
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the 6th International Conference on Pervasive Computing and Social Networking, ICPCSN 2026
SP  - 1189
EP  - 1194
DO  - 10.1109/ICPCSN68523.2026.11543532
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042563343?origin=resultslist
AD  - Mohan Babu University, Dept. Of Cse, Andhra Pradesh, Tirupati, India
AD  - Ramachandra College Of Engineering, Dept. Of Ai And Ds, Andhra Pradesh, Eluru, India
AB  - Social engineering remains one of the most operationally consequential attack categories in cybersecurity, exploiting human behaviour rather than software vulnerabilities to achieve initial access. Quick Response (QR) codes have emerged as a delivery mechanism for such attacks a technique termed "quishing"because the encoded payload is visually opaque and therefore bypasses the perceptual inspection that users apply to hyperlinks. Quishing campaigns have been documented across financial-sector intrusions, enterprise credential-harvesting operations, and public-infrastructure attacks in which malicious codes are physically substituted for legitimate ones on payment terminals and official signage. Existing defences address this threat class along a single analysis axis. Image-based classifiers identify structural tampering in the QR matrix but cannot evaluate the content of structurally conformant codes. URL-reputation systems assess the decoded payload but collapse against freshly registered domains and redirect chains routing through intermediate clean-reputation hops. Neither architecture closes both attack surfaces simultaneously. We propose the Hybrid LLM-Vision (HLLM-V) Pipeline, a dual-channel detection framework that treats structural and semantic evidence as independent, concurrently processed streams. An attention-augmented EfficientNet-B3 evaluates image-level structural integrity; a LoRA-fine-tuned LLaMA 2-7B language model performs semantic threat analysis over the decoded payload enriched with WHOIS registration age and a reconstructed redirect-chain transcript. A trainable score-level fusion layer, supplemented by a feature-level MLP branch, integrates both streams into a calibrated threat probability. On 4,200 annotated QR images the system yields 98.4% accuracy and 94.2% accuracy under FGSM adversarial perturbation, exceeding the vision-only baseline by 11.8 percentage points. © 2026 IEEE.
