AD  - Potti Sriramulu Chalavadhi Mallikarjuna Rao College of Engineering & Technology, Department of Computer Science and Engineering (IoT & Cs Incl. BCT), A.P, Vijayawada, India
AB  - The fire accident scenarios result in a challenging working environment for the firefighters due to dense smoke clouds, extreme temperatures above 600°C, and presence of lethal gases. Hence, it becomes challenging for the firefighters to detect the persons trapped inside the building. Traditionally designed firefighting gear mainly concentrates on providing physical safety but lacks the capability to provide real-time intelligent monitoring. This paper presents an experimental study on the design, development, and analysis of an IoT-based smart helmet equipped with human detection using the YOLOv8 machine learning algorithm, multi-sensor environmental monitoring, dual-buzzer alert system, live video streaming, and GPS location tracking capability. The proposed solution utilizes the Arduino UNO microcontroller as a central processing unit integrated with temperature and humidity sensor (DHT11), gas sensors (MQ-2), IR sensors, light intensity sensors, GPS sensor (NEO-6M), and ESP32-CAM camera to capture images and stream live videos. The proposed YOLOv8 model detects humans within the fire environment with 95.2% accuracy with precision rate of 94.8% and recall rate of 95.6%. The MQ-2 gas sensor detects any kind of lethal gas with less than 3 seconds delay. Implementation of the prototype proved to be technically feasible and user-friendly. Potential areas for further development will focus on incorporating thermal imaging technology, utilizing LoRa mesh networks, and conducting trials with functional fire brigades.  © 2026 IEEE.
