SP  - 98
EP  - 104
DO  - 10.1109/ICICDS70526.2026.11604835
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046076903?origin=resultslist
AD  - Srm Institute Of Science And Technology, Department Of Electronics And Communication Engineering, Tamil Nadu, India
AB  - Monitoring maritime boundaries and Exclusive Economic Zones (EEZs) is critically hindered by the vast scale of ocean environments, the prohibitive costs of satellite-based surveillance (()10,000-()50,000/ month), and the high false-alarm rates of GPS-only geofencing systems. This paper presents the design, implementation, and evaluation of an edge-intelligent, autonomous buoy network for decentralized maritime border security. Each solar-powered buoy node integrates a GPS module, a six-axis IMU (MPU6050), and a waterproof ultrasonic sensor, communicating via an infrastructure-independent LoRa mesh network at 4 3 3 ~ M H z. The core theoretical contribution, the Inverse Wave Buoy Analogy, exploits stationary buoy IMU data to detect anomalous hydrodynamic disturbances caused by approaching vessels inverting the conventional ship-mounted wave estimation paradigm. A two-stage XGBoost ensemble processes sensor data: WakeNet performs four-class wake disturbance classification, and FusionNet delivers binary boundary-violation detection. ExplainNet applies SHAP-based explainability to generate real-time, sensor-level alert narratives. Evaluated on a 1 0, 0 0 0-sample structured dataset with realistic noise, WakeNet achieves 96.65% accuracy (CV: 98.40 % pm 0.73 %) and FusionNet achieves 95.55% accuracy (CV: 99.60 % pm 0.49 %, ROC-AUC: 1.0), with a false-negative rate of only 0.62%. Results stream at near-zero latency to a live GIS dashboard via WebSocket. © 2026 IEEE.
